1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:02,376
Illinois Extension puts it plainly:

2
00:00:02,460 --> 00:00:07,020
lettuce and herbs usually need six
to eight hours of bright light every day.

3
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:10,396
Now look at your kitchen
window in December.

4
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:13,520
How many hours of bright
light does it really give you?

5
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:18,476
That one number decides whether
your winter garden becomes fresh salad

6
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:22,720
or a tray of pale, stretched out
seedlings leaning toward the glass.

7
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:25,380
Here are seven vegetables.

8
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:29,016
Five can grow with a bright
window and a few honest limits.

9
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:31,156
Two of them, tomatoes and peppers,

10
00:00:31,240 --> 00:00:33,180
need extra light in winter.

11
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:35,856
And there's one quiet sign,

12
00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:37,856
weeks before a plant gives up,

13
00:00:37,940 --> 00:00:39,876
that your window isn't enough.

14
00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:42,020
I'll show you exactly where to look.

15
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:47,216
First, the light itself,
because it explains everything else.

16
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:48,856
Then the seven vegetables.

17
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:50,136
Then one table.

18
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:52,460
Then lamps and a four-week plan.

19
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:55,696
Everything here comes
from university extension offices,

20
00:00:55,780 --> 00:00:57,696
and the list is in the description.

21
00:00:57,780 --> 00:01:00,360
Where those sources go
quiet, I'll tell you.

22
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:03,036
Picture a December afternoon.

23
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:05,556
The sun reaches the glass
for a little while,

24
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:07,256
slides across the sill,

25
00:01:07,340 --> 00:01:09,736
and then it's gone behind
the neighbor's roof.

26
00:01:09,820 --> 00:01:12,496
The tray on that sill
barely got a day at all.

27
00:01:12,580 --> 00:01:14,460
It just sits there in the dim.

28
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:17,576
Here's the thing about
plants and weak light.

29
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:18,976
They don't wilt first.

30
00:01:19,060 --> 00:01:20,136
They stretch.

31
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:21,376
Extension guides point

32
00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:25,496
out that a windowsill can be enough
for seeds that need light to sprout.

33
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:26,896
But once they sprout,

34
00:01:26,980 --> 00:01:29,096
they stretch if the light is too weak,

35
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:30,376
tall, thin,

36
00:01:30,460 --> 00:01:33,320
and reaching for the glass
like a kid on tiptoe.

37
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:35,896
That's the quiet sign.

38
00:01:35,980 --> 00:01:39,696
If your seedlings are getting taller
and thinner while the leaves stay small,

39
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:41,476
they're asking for more light.

40
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:42,516
Don't panic.

41
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:43,816
It's an easy fix,

42
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:46,100
and I'll show you how in a few minutes.

43
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:49,176
And stretching is only the first warning.

44
00:01:49,260 --> 00:01:50,856
For vegetables that make fruit,

45
00:01:50,940 --> 00:01:52,576
too little light means tall,

46
00:01:52,660 --> 00:01:56,076
spindly plants and flowers
that never set fruit.

47
00:01:56,160 --> 00:01:57,876
So the stretching comes first.

48
00:01:57,960 --> 00:01:59,960
The flowers just never come.

49
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:03,416
So how much light does
a winter window give?

50
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:04,896
Here's the honest answer.

51
00:02:04,980 --> 00:02:08,976
One Illinois houseplant guide calls
a south-facing window the best option

52
00:02:09,060 --> 00:02:11,136
at about three hundred foot candles.

53
00:02:11,220 --> 00:02:14,576
That's just a unit for measuring light,
and you don't need to measure it.

54
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:19,076
But that guide gives no winter hours,
and neither does any other source I found.

55
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:21,200
So I won't invent a number for you.

56
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:23,876
Here's what you can do instead.

57
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:24,876
Tomorrow,

58
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:27,276
tape a sheet of paper next to the window.

59
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:28,216
Each hour,

60
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:30,356
write sun or no sun.

61
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:32,156
One day is enough.

62
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:33,356
That's your own number,

63
00:02:33,440 --> 00:02:35,696
and it beats any number I could hand you.

64
00:02:35,780 --> 00:02:36,876
Keep that sheet.

65
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:38,500
We'll use it at the end.

66
00:02:39,580 --> 00:02:43,916
Now, what the universities do
agree on, a bright sunny window,

67
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:45,636
best if it faces south,

68
00:02:45,720 --> 00:02:48,731
is almost a must for growing
vegetables indoors.

69
00:02:48,815 --> 00:02:50,851
Leaves usually forgive weak light.

70
00:02:50,935 --> 00:02:52,555
Fruit often doesn't.

71
00:02:53,415 --> 00:02:55,011
Here's why that matters.

72
00:02:55,095 --> 00:02:57,455
It splits this list into two groups.

73
00:02:57,575 --> 00:02:59,711
The leafy crops can try your window.

74
00:02:59,795 --> 00:03:01,611
The fruiting crops need a lamp.

75
00:03:01,695 --> 00:03:04,731
Know which group a plant
belongs to before you buy seeds,

76
00:03:04,815 --> 00:03:07,395
and you'll save yourself
a lot of wasted soil.

77
00:03:08,255 --> 00:03:09,811
Keep one number in your head,

78
00:03:09,895 --> 00:03:11,711
like a song you can't shake.

79
00:03:11,795 --> 00:03:13,231
Six to eight hours.

80
00:03:13,315 --> 00:03:15,015
We'll keep coming back to it.

81
00:03:15,755 --> 00:03:17,551
One more thing before we start.

82
00:03:17,635 --> 00:03:21,571
Two plants on this list need a stretch
of darkness at night to do their job,

83
00:03:21,655 --> 00:03:23,391
and only one of them does.

84
00:03:23,475 --> 00:03:25,731
Tomatoes and peppers don't agree on that,

85
00:03:25,815 --> 00:03:27,831
and it changes how you set a lamp.

86
00:03:27,915 --> 00:03:29,735
I'll get to it in a few minutes.

87
00:03:30,915 --> 00:03:33,391
Let's start with the fastest
thing on this list:

88
00:03:33,475 --> 00:03:34,755
microgreens.

89
00:03:35,615 --> 00:03:37,591
Maryland Extension says microgreens are

90
00:03:37,675 --> 00:03:40,271
ready seven to twenty-
one days after sowing,

91
00:03:40,355 --> 00:03:42,331
when they're two to three inches tall.

92
00:03:42,415 --> 00:03:44,071
That's the quickest harvest here,

93
00:03:44,155 --> 00:03:45,535
by a long way.

94
00:03:46,355 --> 00:03:48,611
You sow them thickly in a shallow tray,

95
00:03:48,695 --> 00:03:50,531
and you cut them with clean scissors,

96
00:03:50,615 --> 00:03:52,211
just above the soil line,

97
00:03:52,295 --> 00:03:54,311
once the first true leaves appear.

98
00:03:54,395 --> 00:03:56,691
Here's a small trick from the same guide.

99
00:03:56,775 --> 00:03:59,971
Keep the tray covered for a few
days after the seeds sprout,

100
00:04:00,055 --> 00:04:01,691
and the stems come up longer,

101
00:04:01,775 --> 00:04:03,415
which makes cutting easier.

102
00:04:04,095 --> 00:04:05,691
Now, the honest limit.

103
00:04:05,775 --> 00:04:08,851
Light from a sunny window
may be enough in some cases,

104
00:04:08,935 --> 00:04:12,171
but microgreens generally
grow best under grow lights.

105
00:04:12,255 --> 00:04:15,111
So think of them as a try
the window first crop.

106
00:04:15,195 --> 00:04:17,855
If they start leaning
and thinning, add a lamp.

107
00:04:18,555 --> 00:04:21,371
It takes a minute to set
up and no special gear.

108
00:04:21,455 --> 00:04:25,135
All you need is a shallow tray,
some potting mix, and seeds.

109
00:04:26,055 --> 00:04:27,331
Microgreens are quick,

110
00:04:27,415 --> 00:04:28,695
but they're tiny.

111
00:04:28,795 --> 00:04:31,371
If you want something you
can cut again and again,

112
00:04:31,455 --> 00:04:33,275
the next group is for you.

113
00:04:34,815 --> 00:04:37,531
Arugula, mustard greens, and Asian greens.

114
00:04:37,615 --> 00:04:41,391
These are baby greens, ready twenty-
one to forty days after sowing,

115
00:04:41,475 --> 00:04:43,411
when they're four to six inches tall.

116
00:04:43,495 --> 00:04:44,891
And here's the part I like.

117
00:04:44,975 --> 00:04:47,855
They can regrow for one
or two more harvests.

118
00:04:48,735 --> 00:04:49,891
You cut the leaves,

119
00:04:49,975 --> 00:04:51,091
you leave the plant,

120
00:04:51,175 --> 00:04:52,811
and it gives you another round.

121
00:04:52,895 --> 00:04:54,751
Same pot, same seeds,

122
00:04:54,835 --> 00:04:56,015
more salad.

123
00:04:56,795 --> 00:04:58,391
And this is the forgiving group.

124
00:04:58,475 --> 00:04:59,871
Lettuce, arugula,

125
00:04:59,955 --> 00:05:04,691
mustards, and Asian greens will grow
acceptably well in less than ideal light.

126
00:05:04,775 --> 00:05:05,831
Not great light.

127
00:05:05,915 --> 00:05:07,015
Acceptable.

128
00:05:07,135 --> 00:05:08,471
On a gray winter sill,

129
00:05:08,555 --> 00:05:10,395
acceptable is a big deal.

130
00:05:11,155 --> 00:05:15,131
So if you only plant one thing from this
video and your window isn't perfect,

131
00:05:15,215 --> 00:05:16,615
make it this group.

132
00:05:17,415 --> 00:05:18,831
That's two of seven.

133
00:05:18,915 --> 00:05:21,251
Next is the one everybody asks about:

134
00:05:21,335 --> 00:05:22,255
lettuce.

135
00:05:23,395 --> 00:05:28,551
Illinois Extension says loose leaf lettuce
can be harvested at about four weeks.

136
00:05:28,635 --> 00:05:30,851
Four weeks from seed to salad bowl,

137
00:05:30,935 --> 00:05:34,155
and that first crisp bite
feels like a small win.

138
00:05:35,035 --> 00:05:35,951
Now the catch,

139
00:05:36,035 --> 00:05:37,351
and it's about your window.

140
00:05:37,435 --> 00:05:41,511
Lettuce wants a very sunny spot
and that six to eight hour number again.

141
00:05:41,595 --> 00:05:44,751
So if your window faces south,
try the sill first.

142
00:05:44,835 --> 00:05:47,791
If it faces north or a big tree blocks it,

143
00:05:47,875 --> 00:05:49,811
plan on a lamp from day one.

144
00:05:49,895 --> 00:05:50,811
If that's you,

145
00:05:50,895 --> 00:05:52,431
the lamp isn't a luxury,

146
00:05:52,515 --> 00:05:53,755
it's the plan.

147
00:05:54,635 --> 00:05:56,411
Lettuce likes it cool too.

148
00:05:56,495 --> 00:05:58,051
Seventy degrees or below,

149
00:05:58,135 --> 00:05:59,671
away from direct heat.

150
00:05:59,755 --> 00:06:04,715
That cool back bedroom nobody uses
might be the best salad bar in the house.

151
00:06:05,635 --> 00:06:10,331
Leaf lettuce and a small butterhead
called Tom Thumb are good ones to try.

152
00:06:10,415 --> 00:06:13,295
Pick a leaf type and start with one tray.

153
00:06:13,995 --> 00:06:15,211
That's three of seven,

154
00:06:15,295 --> 00:06:16,691
and when we get to the table,

155
00:06:16,775 --> 00:06:19,771
I'll show you three places
where the universities disagree,

156
00:06:19,855 --> 00:06:22,471
including one about
washing your microgreens.

157
00:06:22,555 --> 00:06:23,491
But first,

158
00:06:23,575 --> 00:06:25,735
a pair that asks for patience.

159
00:06:26,895 --> 00:06:28,471
Chives and parsley.

160
00:06:28,555 --> 00:06:29,831
These are the slow ones,

161
00:06:29,915 --> 00:06:31,715
so let's be upfront about it.

162
00:06:32,535 --> 00:06:36,091
Chives take about twelve weeks
from seed to the first cutting.

163
00:06:36,175 --> 00:06:38,211
Parsley takes ten to twelve.

164
00:06:38,295 --> 00:06:39,871
That's nearly three months.

165
00:06:39,955 --> 00:06:42,371
So this isn't a salad next week crop.

166
00:06:42,455 --> 00:06:43,751
It's a plant it now,

167
00:06:43,835 --> 00:06:45,995
snip it in the dead of winter crop.

168
00:06:46,775 --> 00:06:50,371
The good news is that chives prefer
cool conditions with good light,

169
00:06:50,455 --> 00:06:53,271
and they grow quite well
on a kitchen windowsill.

170
00:06:53,355 --> 00:06:55,611
Plant the seeds in a six-inch pot.

171
00:06:55,695 --> 00:06:56,691
For parsley,

172
00:06:56,775 --> 00:06:59,255
one vigorous plant per pot is plenty.

173
00:07:00,075 --> 00:07:01,191
Here's the trade.

174
00:07:01,275 --> 00:07:02,231
You wait longer,

175
00:07:02,315 --> 00:07:06,571
and in return you get a pot you can snip
a few sprigs from whenever you're cooking.

176
00:07:06,655 --> 00:07:08,371
Patience pays on this one,

177
00:07:08,455 --> 00:07:11,855
and there's real comfort
in a green pot waiting on the sill.

178
00:07:12,695 --> 00:07:14,051
That's four of seven.

179
00:07:14,135 --> 00:07:17,155
The next one is where
the sources get very quiet.

180
00:07:18,355 --> 00:07:19,251
Radishes.

181
00:07:19,335 --> 00:07:22,435
Here's exactly what
the sources say and no more.

182
00:07:23,275 --> 00:07:26,215
Radishes are a quick
growing crop for indoors.

183
00:07:26,315 --> 00:07:29,511
Scatter the seeds on moist
soil in a six-inch pot.

184
00:07:29,595 --> 00:07:33,075
They have to grow very rapidly if
they're to be crisp and succulent.

185
00:07:33,834 --> 00:07:35,591
And here's what I could not find.

186
00:07:35,675 --> 00:07:40,211
No extension source I found gives
a number of days or hours of light

187
00:07:40,295 --> 00:07:42,391
for radishes grown indoors.

188
00:07:42,475 --> 00:07:45,051
Some videos online give exact figures.

189
00:07:45,135 --> 00:07:47,735
I can't back them up,
so I won't repeat them.

190
00:07:47,875 --> 00:07:49,711
Treat radish as a test.

191
00:07:49,795 --> 00:07:51,911
One small pot, your brightest sill,

192
00:07:51,995 --> 00:07:53,395
and see what you get.

193
00:07:54,295 --> 00:07:55,211
That's five.

194
00:07:55,295 --> 00:07:58,731
And so far, you haven't needed
to buy anything but seeds and a tray.

195
00:07:58,815 --> 00:08:01,275
The next two are where the lamp comes in.

196
00:08:02,415 --> 00:08:03,571
Tomatoes first.

197
00:08:03,655 --> 00:08:05,671
The small ones can work indoors.

198
00:08:05,755 --> 00:08:06,589
One

199
00:08:06,791 --> 00:08:10,107
Tiny Tim, Small Fry,
and a paste tomato called Roma

200
00:08:10,191 --> 00:08:12,911
can be raised quite
satisfactorily at home.

201
00:08:13,011 --> 00:08:16,227
Tiny Tim stays around twelve
to fifteen inches tall.

202
00:08:16,311 --> 00:08:18,447
Small Fry is closer to three feet,

203
00:08:18,531 --> 00:08:19,827
so give it more room,

204
00:08:19,911 --> 00:08:21,187
like a sunroom.

205
00:08:21,271 --> 00:08:26,687
Micro Tom, Terenzo, and Lozano are other
names to look for. For the smallest kinds,

206
00:08:26,771 --> 00:08:28,851
a one-gallon pot is enough.

207
00:08:29,811 --> 00:08:30,847
Now the light.

208
00:08:30,931 --> 00:08:34,527
New Hampshire Extension says
fruiting vegetables like tomatoes

209
00:08:34,611 --> 00:08:37,247
need supplemental light
during the winter months.

210
00:08:37,331 --> 00:08:42,207
Without it, you get tall, spindly
plants that fail to flower and set fruit.

211
00:08:42,291 --> 00:08:46,127
Keep the tops of the plants close
to a cool white fluorescent light.

212
00:08:46,211 --> 00:08:48,591
A south or west window helps too.

213
00:08:49,431 --> 00:08:51,407
And here's the surprise I promised.

214
00:08:51,491 --> 00:08:53,007
Tomatoes need darkness,

215
00:08:53,091 --> 00:08:55,347
about six hours of dark to fruit.

216
00:08:55,431 --> 00:08:58,547
Some tomatoes are being bred
to handle round-the-clock light.

217
00:08:58,631 --> 00:09:00,287
But for what you'd plant at home,

218
00:09:00,371 --> 00:09:01,807
plan on that dark break.

219
00:09:01,891 --> 00:09:03,207
Call it beauty sleep.

220
00:09:03,291 --> 00:09:05,751
Peppers, as we'll see
in a second, are different.

221
00:09:06,571 --> 00:09:08,187
Here's why this matters.

222
00:09:08,271 --> 00:09:11,307
A lamp that never turns
off sounds generous,

223
00:09:11,391 --> 00:09:13,847
but for a tomato, it can work against you.

224
00:09:13,931 --> 00:09:18,327
So use a timer and let the lamp go off
for at least six hours every night.

225
00:09:18,411 --> 00:09:19,391
That's it.

226
00:09:20,151 --> 00:09:21,487
Two cautions:

227
00:09:21,571 --> 00:09:24,727
temperatures below fifty
degrees can harm tomatoes,

228
00:09:24,811 --> 00:09:28,411
and temperatures above ninety
may keep fruit from setting.

229
00:09:28,531 --> 00:09:31,111
And watch for whiteflies and aphids.

230
00:09:32,351 --> 00:09:33,427
Then there's the wait.

231
00:09:33,511 --> 00:09:37,387
Tomatoes and peppers are ready
to harvest about ten weeks after planting.

232
00:09:37,471 --> 00:09:38,567
Ten weeks of lamp,

233
00:09:38,651 --> 00:09:40,307
timer, and patience.

234
00:09:40,391 --> 00:09:41,927
If that doesn't sound like you,

235
00:09:42,011 --> 00:09:43,007
leave this one out.

236
00:09:43,091 --> 00:09:44,691
Nobody's keeping score.

237
00:09:45,751 --> 00:09:47,667
Peppers are the other lamp crop.

238
00:09:47,751 --> 00:09:50,807
Small-fruited peppers can
be grown as indoor plants,

239
00:09:50,891 --> 00:09:52,147
and like tomatoes,

240
00:09:52,231 --> 00:09:54,547
they need warm, bright conditions.

241
00:09:54,631 --> 00:09:56,547
Same lamp rule applies.

242
00:09:56,631 --> 00:09:58,307
Supplemental light in winter,

243
00:09:58,391 --> 00:10:01,291
or you get tall, thin plants and no fruit.

244
00:10:02,131 --> 00:10:03,427
But here's the difference.

245
00:10:03,511 --> 00:10:05,747
Tomatoes need that stretch of dark.

246
00:10:05,831 --> 00:10:09,731
Peppers do not. So peppers
don't need the same dark break.

247
00:10:10,631 --> 00:10:12,807
Harvest comes in at about ten weeks,

248
00:10:12,891 --> 00:10:14,251
same as tomatoes,

249
00:10:14,391 --> 00:10:17,091
and the same two pests may show up.

250
00:10:17,831 --> 00:10:19,227
I'll say this plainly.

251
00:10:19,311 --> 00:10:23,587
The sources I found don't name
pepper varieties for indoor growing.

252
00:10:23,671 --> 00:10:28,151
So choose a small-fruited type
and check the seed packet for size.

253
00:10:29,291 --> 00:10:30,347
That's all seven.

254
00:10:30,431 --> 00:10:32,431
Now let's put them in one place.

255
00:10:33,551 --> 00:10:35,367
Here's the table on your screen.

256
00:10:35,451 --> 00:10:37,687
Seven crops side by side.

257
00:10:37,771 --> 00:10:42,455
It's the one thing in this video you
won't find in any single university guide,

258
00:10:42,539 --> 00:10:45,019
because I lined up their numbers myself.

259
00:10:45,859 --> 00:10:47,395
You don’t need me to read it out.

260
00:10:47,479 --> 00:10:49,035
Read it as two columns.

261
00:10:49,119 --> 00:10:51,235
On the left, the leafy crops,

262
00:10:51,319 --> 00:10:52,715
which cope with a window,

263
00:10:52,799 --> 00:10:54,975
and a few of them do better with a lamp.

264
00:10:55,059 --> 00:10:57,375
On the right, tomatoes and peppers,

265
00:10:57,459 --> 00:10:59,235
which don’t get a choice in winter.

266
00:10:59,319 --> 00:11:01,455
And that blank square next to radish?

267
00:11:01,539 --> 00:11:03,035
It’s blank on purpose.

268
00:11:03,119 --> 00:11:04,535
The sources don’t say,

269
00:11:04,619 --> 00:11:06,259
so the table doesn’t either.

270
00:11:07,539 --> 00:11:10,155
Now, the part I find most interesting.

271
00:11:10,239 --> 00:11:13,759
Here are three places where
the universities disagree.

272
00:11:14,679 --> 00:11:17,255
First, how many hours of light?

273
00:11:17,339 --> 00:11:21,379
Illinois says lettuce and herbs need
six to eight hours of bright light.

274
00:11:21,519 --> 00:11:26,095
Maryland says the total should be about
sixteen hours a day from all sources.

275
00:11:26,179 --> 00:11:27,315
Those look different,

276
00:11:27,399 --> 00:11:28,759
but read the wording.

277
00:11:28,859 --> 00:11:31,155
One is about the bright
light a plant needs.

278
00:11:31,239 --> 00:11:33,895
The other counts every
hour from every source,

279
00:11:33,979 --> 00:11:35,279
lamp included.

280
00:11:36,159 --> 00:11:38,695
Second, how far should the lamp be?

281
00:11:38,779 --> 00:11:40,955
Illinois says six to twelve inches.

282
00:11:41,039 --> 00:11:43,059
New Hampshire says four to six.

283
00:11:43,159 --> 00:11:45,995
Utah State says three to six for tomatoes.

284
00:11:46,079 --> 00:11:48,575
I couldn’t find a source
that explains the gap.

285
00:11:48,659 --> 00:11:50,835
So here’s the rule that
works with all of them.

286
00:11:50,919 --> 00:11:52,555
Start where your guide says.

287
00:11:52,639 --> 00:11:53,975
If the plants stretch,

288
00:11:54,059 --> 00:11:55,155
lower the lamp.

289
00:11:55,239 --> 00:11:56,275
If they burn,

290
00:11:56,359 --> 00:11:57,279
raise it.

291
00:11:58,139 --> 00:12:01,355
Third, should you wash
microgreens after cutting?

292
00:12:01,439 --> 00:12:03,575
Maryland says to rinse them with water,

293
00:12:03,659 --> 00:12:04,655
pat them dry,

294
00:12:04,739 --> 00:12:06,115
and use them right away,

295
00:12:06,199 --> 00:12:08,755
or keep them in the fridge
for up to ten days.

296
00:12:08,839 --> 00:12:12,195
Penn State says post-harvest
washing is not recommended

297
00:12:12,279 --> 00:12:14,195
unless absolutely necessary,

298
00:12:14,279 --> 00:12:16,479
because it can spread contamination.

299
00:12:16,599 --> 00:12:17,775
One thing to know:

300
00:12:17,859 --> 00:12:21,935
Penn State’s guidance is framed around
commercial microgreens producers.

301
00:12:22,019 --> 00:12:24,055
Both say to use clean water.

302
00:12:24,139 --> 00:12:25,515
So if you grow at home,

303
00:12:25,599 --> 00:12:27,435
ask your local extension office,

304
00:12:27,519 --> 00:12:29,659
and use clean water either way.

305
00:12:30,799 --> 00:12:32,055
That’s the table.

306
00:12:32,139 --> 00:12:33,655
Now, lamps.

307
00:12:33,739 --> 00:12:35,699
And you don’t need a fancy one.

308
00:12:36,419 --> 00:12:40,595
A simple setup is two forty-
watt cool white fluorescent tubes

309
00:12:40,679 --> 00:12:42,514
a few inches above the plants,

310
00:12:42,598 --> 00:12:45,159
running fourteen to sixteen hours a day.

311
00:12:45,259 --> 00:12:48,675
The newer T5 tubes are more
efficient than the older types,

312
00:12:48,759 --> 00:12:51,295
and LED shop lamps will work fine.

313
00:12:51,379 --> 00:12:55,299
They use less energy than fluorescent
lights for the same amount of light.

314
00:12:56,039 --> 00:12:57,735
Here’s where the experts agree.

315
00:12:57,819 --> 00:13:00,935
They word it differently,
but they land on one idea.

316
00:13:01,019 --> 00:13:02,355
Keep the light close,

317
00:13:02,439 --> 00:13:03,955
and let it run long.

318
00:13:04,039 --> 00:13:07,075
Close means a few inches
from the tops of the plants.

319
00:13:07,159 --> 00:13:09,079
Long means most of the day.

320
00:13:09,199 --> 00:13:11,499
Then let the plant tell you what it wants.

321
00:13:11,599 --> 00:13:12,635
If it stretches,

322
00:13:12,719 --> 00:13:14,855
lower the lamp or run it longer.

323
00:13:14,939 --> 00:13:16,135
If the leaves burn,

324
00:13:16,219 --> 00:13:17,055
raise it.

325
00:13:17,139 --> 00:13:18,639
That’s the whole system.

326
00:13:18,759 --> 00:13:20,719
Nudge it like tuning a radio.

327
00:13:21,759 --> 00:13:22,695
Use a timer.

328
00:13:22,779 --> 00:13:25,943
It takes a minute to set,
and then you never have to remember.

329
00:13:26,763 --> 00:13:27,899
How many hours?

330
00:13:27,983 --> 00:13:28,959
For seedlings,

331
00:13:29,043 --> 00:13:33,099
sixteen hours of supplemental light,
with eighteen as the maximum.

332
00:13:33,183 --> 00:13:34,359
And for tomatoes,

333
00:13:34,443 --> 00:13:36,399
remember the six hours of dark.

334
00:13:36,483 --> 00:13:38,963
So use the guide that matches your plant.

335
00:13:39,763 --> 00:13:41,479
There's one more rule of thumb,

336
00:13:41,563 --> 00:13:45,259
about two hours of lamp light
for every hour of natural light.

337
00:13:45,343 --> 00:13:48,739
That comes from one short
guide from twenty seventeen,

338
00:13:48,823 --> 00:13:50,159
and it's a rule of thumb,

339
00:13:50,243 --> 00:13:51,699
not a measured law.

340
00:13:51,783 --> 00:13:53,503
Use it as a starting point.

341
00:13:54,283 --> 00:13:56,979
Two things I'm leaving
out on purpose: prices,

342
00:13:57,063 --> 00:14:00,479
because I haven't found a neutral
source for what a lamp should cost;

343
00:14:00,563 --> 00:14:01,779
and brand names,

344
00:14:01,863 --> 00:14:04,743
because none of the extension
guides recommend one.

345
00:14:05,843 --> 00:14:08,239
Three problems come up again and again,

346
00:14:08,323 --> 00:14:09,939
and every one has a calm,

347
00:14:10,023 --> 00:14:11,243
simple fix.

348
00:14:12,023 --> 00:14:13,819
Problem one, stretching.

349
00:14:13,903 --> 00:14:15,199
We covered the cause.

350
00:14:15,283 --> 00:14:19,023
The fix is to lower the lamp,
run it longer, or both.

351
00:14:19,923 --> 00:14:21,059
Problem two,

352
00:14:21,143 --> 00:14:22,499
mold on the tray.

353
00:14:22,583 --> 00:14:24,899
Make sure there's good air circulation

354
00:14:24,983 --> 00:14:29,379
and run a gentle fan breeze across
dense plantings like microgreens.

355
00:14:29,463 --> 00:14:31,863
Wash trays before you reuse them.

356
00:14:32,663 --> 00:14:33,799
Problem three,

357
00:14:33,883 --> 00:14:35,299
tiny pests.

358
00:14:35,383 --> 00:14:37,759
New Hampshire says whiteflies and aphids

359
00:14:37,843 --> 00:14:40,739
can show up on indoor
tomatoes and peppers.

360
00:14:40,823 --> 00:14:43,379
Yellow sticky traps catch whiteflies.

361
00:14:43,463 --> 00:14:44,419
For aphids,

362
00:14:44,503 --> 00:14:49,339
insecticidal soap or another product
approved for vegetables can be used.

363
00:14:49,423 --> 00:14:51,059
If you use anything like that,

364
00:14:51,143 --> 00:14:52,319
read the label.

365
00:14:52,403 --> 00:14:54,319
Make sure it's approved for vegetables.

366
00:14:54,403 --> 00:14:55,639
Follow it closely,

367
00:14:55,723 --> 00:14:57,719
including any wait before harvest,

368
00:14:57,803 --> 00:15:00,199
and store it out
of reach of kids and pets.

369
00:15:00,283 --> 00:15:01,979
And if you have a dog or a cat,

370
00:15:02,063 --> 00:15:06,483
check each plant's name against the ASPCA
list before it goes on a low shelf.

371
00:15:07,383 --> 00:15:11,119
And one word on safety, because it
comes up a lot. Some people want to grow

372
00:15:11,203 --> 00:15:14,119
their own greens because they
worry about store-bought ones.

373
00:15:14,203 --> 00:15:16,679
Nothing I found says homegrown is safer.

374
00:15:16,763 --> 00:15:19,859
The guidance for microgreens
is about keeping things clean,

375
00:15:19,943 --> 00:15:22,019
using water that's free of E. coli,

376
00:15:22,103 --> 00:15:24,039
and not spreading contamination.

377
00:15:24,123 --> 00:15:25,659
So grow it, enjoy it,

378
00:15:25,743 --> 00:15:28,183
and keep your trays and your water clean.

379
00:15:29,623 --> 00:15:32,583
Now let's turn all this
into a gentle little plan.

380
00:15:33,443 --> 00:15:34,379
Week one,

381
00:15:34,463 --> 00:15:37,099
sow microgreens
and a tray of leaf lettuce.

382
00:15:37,183 --> 00:15:39,219
Set up a lamp if your window is weak.

383
00:15:39,303 --> 00:15:41,059
If you want tomatoes or peppers,

384
00:15:41,143 --> 00:15:42,499
start those now too,

385
00:15:42,583 --> 00:15:44,703
because they take about ten weeks.

386
00:15:45,583 --> 00:15:47,083
Weeks two and three,

387
00:15:47,223 --> 00:15:49,619
cut the microgreens with clean scissors.

388
00:15:49,703 --> 00:15:50,619
Week four,

389
00:15:50,703 --> 00:15:52,059
the lettuce is ready.

390
00:15:52,143 --> 00:15:54,403
Chives and parsley keep growing.

391
00:15:55,423 --> 00:15:57,759
And remember that sheet
of paper by the window?

392
00:15:57,843 --> 00:15:58,759
Pull it out.

393
00:15:58,843 --> 00:16:00,199
Count the sun hours.

394
00:16:00,283 --> 00:16:01,879
Compare them to six to eight,

395
00:16:01,963 --> 00:16:04,039
and you'll know which group you belong to.

396
00:16:04,123 --> 00:16:06,499
If you haven't yet, tomorrow's the day.

397
00:16:06,583 --> 00:16:09,239
Write sun or no sun once an hour.

398
00:16:09,323 --> 00:16:10,723
No tools needed.

399
00:16:11,883 --> 00:16:15,719
Picture the morning when you step
into the kitchen and your tray is green.

400
00:16:15,803 --> 00:16:17,980
Outside, everything is gray.

401
00:16:18,064 --> 00:16:21,224
Inside, there's a small
win waiting on the sill.

402
00:16:22,344 --> 00:16:26,360
If you're the kind of gardener who checks
the sky before you check the forecast,

403
00:16:26,444 --> 00:16:27,784
you belong here.

404
00:16:28,604 --> 00:16:30,760
Which way does your kitchen window face,

405
00:16:30,844 --> 00:16:33,500
and how many hours of sun
does it get in winter?

406
00:16:33,584 --> 00:16:35,124
Tell me in the comments.

407
00:16:36,024 --> 00:16:37,860
Pick one, not all seven.

408
00:16:37,944 --> 00:16:39,004
One tray.

409
00:16:39,824 --> 00:16:41,620
Grow a little, save a little,

410
00:16:41,704 --> 00:16:43,180
and have some fun out there.

411
00:16:43,264 --> 00:16:44,329
Happy gardening.

